essive/reflexive discharge or a relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3-metadata-position\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is at relative position 0.906051 in Edmond Rostand’s Cyrano de Bergerac.\",\"supports\":\"Its late position is consistent with the climactic final-scene context, though position alone does not determine core force.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4-background-recognition\",\"source\":\"background_knowledge\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"In the final scene, Roxane recognizes Cyrano’s authorship or animating voice behind the letters and words through which she had loved Christian, while Cyrano is mortally wounded.\",\"supports\":\"This explains the declaration as a belated sincere avowal and life-saving plea, with only weak performative pressure and no need for O.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":true,\"familiarity\":\"extensive\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"contribution\":\"Knowledge of the final recognition and Cyrano’s mortal condition clarifies the addressee, sincerity, belated recognition, and the purpose of “vivez.”\"},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal, while a low P score preserves the slight force of relational recognition. Emotional urgency belongs in contextual delivery and does not require E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The classification applies to the highlighted “Je vous aime,” while treating the adjacent “vivez” as contextual evidence.\"}"
